## 2. Size authority (repo files, copied read-only to `/workspace/pixellab/reference/scale/`)
| File | sha256 (first 16) | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `art/catalogue/scale_chart.json` | f3af0b1140eaa864 | HUMAN 18×42 (14–22 × 40–44), DWARF 20×36, ELF 16×43, CHILD 14×26; LARGE 64×80 |
| `art/reference/DEUS_HUMAN_SCALE_STRIP_V1.png` | 5ce5cefef0a39d7b | Visual yardstick: its human is 42 px (head 12, torso 12, belt 2, legs 16), baseline y=180 |
| `docs/art/DEUS_HUMAN_WORLD_SCALE_STANDARD.md` | c19014d8fd1aa8cb | Anchor x=24, baseline y=47 in the 48 cell; human ~3.0–3.2 heads; dwarf ~2.8; elf ~3.3 |
| `art/catalogue/size_classes.json` | 91bc1fcfcff9ebca | Drawn heights for the six races the chart lacks (7 px per foot) |
| `art/catalogue/geometry.json` | n/a | Frame classes: SMALL/MEDIUM = 48×48; the TALL_MEDIUM 48×64 option exists but is disabled; small-race readability floor 26–28 px exists but is disabled |
| `art/reference/DEUS_SCALE_LANGUAGE_V1.png` | 7ccbc8d9b67bd736 | Proportion/feel comparison only (not a ruler, per the standard) |
| (derived) `reference/scale/SIZE_BLOCK_HUMAN42_48cell.png` | bb1144b4be083372 | The strip's human cut to a 48 cell (42×18, feet on row 47); post-process overlay and contact sheets |

**How the size reference enters the pipeline:**
- PixelLab has no size-reference input.
- `create-character-v3`'s only image input is `reference_image`, and the model *rotates* that image. It copies the image, so we do not pass the strip there.
- The size reference is therefore used in three places:
  - **(a) Prompt numbers.** Every prompt carries the chart numbers (§4 slots).
  - **(b) Post-process gate.** Height/width/anchor are checked against the chart row (§3 step 7).
  - **(c) Review.** Every contact sheet shows SIZE_BLOCK_HUMAN42 beside the sprite on the same 1x ground.
- **Log per attempt:** `size_ref_files` = [scale_chart.json@f3af0b11 row id or size_classes.json@91bc1fcf race id, DEUS_HUMAN_SCALE_STRIP_V1.png@5ce5cefe].

## 3. Step-by-step process (one step per stage)
Global settings for every call:
- Base URL `https://api.pixellab.ai/v2`.
- `no_background` true.
- `view` "low top-down".
- `template_id` "mannequin".
- `outline` "single color outline" (self-tinted; never "single color black outline").
- `detail` "medium detail".
- `enhance_prompt` **false** (it rewrites the text and drops the pixel numbers).
- Seed `crc32("{race}_{sex}_{class}_{stage}") % 2^31 + 1000 × attempt`.

Budget guard: pre-check `balance − expected_cost ≥ hard stop` before every call. Never print the API key.

### Step 0. Preflight (0 gens)
- **Inputs:** race row (§6), class row (§5), sex, the palette file `art/palette/deus_master_world_palette_v1.hex` (sha befb4641…), and the ramps in §6/§5.
- **Do:**
  - Build the prompt strings from the templates (§4) by code, never by hand.
  - Build `pal_{race}_{class}.png`: all ramp colours plus the outline shades, ≤40 colours [TUNE].
  - Write the manifest row.
- **Accept:** every slot is filled, and no forbidden words appear ("16-bit", "JRPG", "transparent", "sprite sheet", "paper-doll", "background").

### Step 1. Base character
- **Endpoint:** `POST /create-character-v3`, from scratch (no `reference_image`).
- **Settings:**
  - `description` = Template B (§4.1).
  - `image_size` {C,C} with C = the race canvas (§6): human 44.
  - Global settings as above. `name` = `{race}_{sex}_{class}_base`.
- **Cost:** ~2 gens at C ≤ 64 (measured 2.0–2.05 ×3).
- **Output:** 8 rotations. GET `/characters/{id}` and download `rotation_urls` with `curl -sfL -A 'Mozilla/5.0'`.
- **Post:**
  1. Binary-alpha check.
  2. Measure the bbox, feet row and centre column per direction.
  3. Anchor-crop into the 48×48 cell: feet on y=47, bbox centre on x=24, whole pixels only, no scaling.
  4. OKLab no-dither snap to `pal_{race}_{class}`, then save `base_palette.png` (the colours actually used).
- **Accept:**
  - Height within the race min–max in all 8 directions.
  - Height within ±1 of the target in S/W/E/N.
  - Width (torso, excluding weapon) within the chart min–max.
  - Weapon (sheathed/slung) inside the 48 cell.
  - Outline self-tinted: 0 pure-black outline pixels (`#000000`–`#0a0a0a`).
  - ≤40 colours after the snap.
  - Straight-on S view; face readable at 1x.
  - The class silhouette items from §5 are visible in S and N.

### Step 2. Base animations: idle and walk (on the base character)
- **Endpoint:** `POST /characters/animations` `mode` "v3", one direction per call, directions S, W, E, N.
- **Settings:**
  - `character_id` = base; `description` = Template B + that direction's facing phrase.
  - `action_description` = Template A-idle or A-walk.
  - `frame_count`: idle 4, walk 4 (select 3 for RMMZ: 1-2-3-2 playback).
  - `keep_first_frame` false.
  - `color_image` = base_palette.png, `force_colors` true.
  - Seed per policy.
- **Cost:** ~1 gen per direction (measured) → 8 gens.
- **Post:**
  - Frames come back on a larger canvas (44 → 64). Crop the whole clip with **one** offset, computed from the base rotation's feet/centre. Never re-centre per frame.
  - Snap to base_palette.
- **Accept:**
  - Feet row ±1 across frames (a whole-pixel re-anchor on the feet is allowed).
  - Head px exact vs base ≥80 % outside moving parts.
  - 0 colours outside base_palette after the snap.
  - Loops cleanly.

### Step 3. States (5 per character)
- **Endpoint:** `POST /create-character-state`, the API equivalent of the web "Create State" screen.
- **Settings:**
  - `character_id` = base.
  - `edit_description` = Template S-{state} (§4.2).
  - `override_frame_size` {CS,CS} with CS = the state canvas (§6): human 56. Always resize, because every state moves limbs or a weapon out of the standing silhouette. Multiples of 4, ≥ C.
  - `use_color_palette_from_reference` **true** for all 5 states: they are pose changes of the same outfit, and gear does not change. Set it **false** only if the Owner approves a state that adds new colours, such as spell glow. Then list the new ramp in the prompt and add it to the palette.
  - `no_background` true; `seed` per policy; `state_name` = "Attack" / "Cast" / "Ranged" / "Down" / "Work".
- **No style input:** the State API has none; the base character is the only reference.
- **Cost:** 20 gens at CS ≤ 84 (web: 20–40); verify on the first call.
- **Post:**
  - Download 8 rotations.
  - Anchor by feet/centre into the 48 cell; for Down, anchor the body's centre of mass on x=24 and its lowest pixel on y=47.
  - **Snap to base_palette.** The look test showed a State redraws with a new ≈26-colour palette (only 1 exact colour shared with the base).
- **Accept:**
  - All opaque pixels fit inside the 48×48 cell after anchoring (frame class MEDIUM/SMALL = 48×48). If a weapon is clipped, retry. After 2 retries, raise the Owner decision O4 (64-px action frames).
  - Figure height ±1 of the base (not for Down).
  - Face, hair and outfit read as the same person; head IoU vs base ≥0.6 (measured 0.57–0.82).
  - Weapon length within ±2 px of §5.
  - The weapon is in the correct hand (character's right = viewer's left in S).

### Step 4. State animations (one per state)
- **Endpoint:** `POST /characters/animations` `mode` "v3", directions S, W, E, N, one per call.
- **Settings:**
  - `character_id` = that State; `description` = Template B + facing phrase.
  - `action_description` = Template A-{state}.
  - `frame_count` 6 for every state clip.
  - `keep_first_frame` false.
  - `color_image` = base_palette.png + `force_colors` true.
  - **Down only:** interpolate from standing with `custom_start_frame` = base rotation (that direction) and `end_frame` = the Down State rotation.
- **Cost:** ~1 gen per direction at ≤64; 1–2 above 64 [TUNE] → 20 gens (5 states × 4).
- **Post:**
  - One crop offset per clip; the 48 cell (or the O4 frame).
  - Snap to base_palette. The look test had 7–15 off-palette colours per frame even with force_colors.
- **Accept:**
  - Feet ±1 (L11 drifted 3 px, so re-anchor).
  - Weapon length ±2 px frame to frame (L11: 13–18 px).
  - Nothing outside the cell.
  - The motion reads at 1x.

### Step 5. Directions for RMMZ
- Use the S/W/E/N rotations and animations; diagonals are kept for DEUS use only.
- No W↔E mirroring, because weapons are handed.

### Step 6. Packing
RMMZ `$` sheets with 48×48 frames:
- Walk: 3 frames × 4 rows per sheet.
- Idle and each state clip: 3–6 frames × 4 rows.
- Layout per `docs/RMMZ_ASSET_SPEC.md` and geometry.json `rmmzCharacterBlocks`.
- The sidecar stores the anchor (24,47) and the hand/weapon anchors.

### Step 7. QA gate and contact sheet (0 gens)
- Build a 1x sheet on grass, dirt, stone and snow, with SIZE_BLOCK_HUMAN42 and the H05 cottage for scale, plus a view-only 3x nearest-neighbour copy.
- Automated checks:
  - Binary alpha.
  - 1:1 grid (no fat pixels).
  - Height/width vs the race row.
  - Anchor ±1.
  - In-palette 100 %.
  - Outline rule.
  - Light check: upper-left mean L > lower-right mean L.
  - Grayscale readability.
- The Owner approves per race (first sprite) and then by sample.

### Retry rule (all stages)
- If an output fails acceptance, retry with the next seed (attempt+1), max **2 retries** per stage.
- A failure on a measurable number (height, weapon length, clipping) may add one corrective sentence to the prompt; log it as `prompt_patch`.
- After 3 failed attempts, stop and flag the item as `NEEDS_OWNER` (hand-fix, O4, or a chart question).
- Never loosen the chart or accept a scaled image.

### Logging fields (one JSON line per attempt, `pixellab/prod/log.jsonl`)
- **Identity:** `asset_id` (`{race}_{sex}_{class}_{stage}[_{anim}_{dir}]`), `race`, `sex`, `class`, `stage`, `attempt`.
- **Request:** `endpoint`, the full `request_body` (base64 images replaced by file path + sha), the `prompt`/`edit_description`/`action_description` text verbatim, `seed`.
- **References:** `size_ref_files` (with sha and row id), `palette_file` (+sha), `source_character_id`, `result_character_id` / `animation_id`.
- **Cost and time:** `gens_cost`, `balance_before`, `balance_after`, `started_ct`, `finished_ct`.
- **Results:** `raw_files[]`, `post_files[]`, measurements (`height[dir]`, `width[dir]`, `feet_row`, `centre_col`, `colours`, `off_palette_px`, `clipped_px`, `head_iou`, `weapon_len`), `accept` (pass/fail and which check), `prompt_patch`, `reviewer`.

## 4. Prompt templates
**Phrasing rule.** Use the L09 phrasing, which produced the preferred look: a short plain noun phrase, "with …", "wearing A, B, C and D", "empty hands".

L09 was generated with prompt enhancement ON. Its enhanced text was:
> A man with short brown hair and a determined expression, wearing a simple undyed linen tunic, a brown leather belt, wool trousers, and leather boots, standing in a steady, neutral pose with a single color outline.

We keep that sentence style and add the pixel block after it. Enhancement is **off** so the numbers survive [TEST O7].

**Coordinates.** Anchors are given as **(dx, dy) from the ground anchor**:
- dx = columns right of the centre column (viewer's right is +).
- dy = rows above the feet baseline.

That makes them valid on any canvas (base C, state CS, or the 48 cell). The canvas rows are also stated for the base.

### 4.1 Template B — base character (`description`)
```
{race_noun} {sex_noun} {class_noun} with {hair_style} {hair_colour_word} hair and {face_trait}, wearing {garment_1}, {garment_2}, {garment_3} and {footwear}, {carried_weapon_phrase}, empty hands.
Low top-down view. Figure {H} px tall and {W} px wide at the shoulders in a {C}x{C} px canvas: top of head on row {C-H}, feet on row {C-1}, body centred on column {C/2}.
Head {h_head} px, torso {h_torso} px, legs {h_legs} px ({heads} heads tall); shoulders at {H-h_head} px above the feet, belt at {h_legs} px above the feet, hands hanging at {dy_hand} px above the feet, {dx_hand} px left and right of centre.
Colours: skin {skin_b} with shadow {skin_s} and highlight {skin_h}; hair {hair_b}, shadow {hair_s}, highlight {hair_h}; {mat_1_name} {m1_b}, shadow {m1_s}, highlight {m1_h}; {mat_2_name} {m2_b}, shadow {m2_s}, highlight {m2_h}; {mat_3_name} {m3_b}, shadow {m3_s}, highlight {m3_h}; {accent_name} {acc_b}, shadow {acc_s}, highlight {acc_h}. At most {N_colours} colours.
{sig_item_1: shape, W x H px, position (dx,dy), material, colour}; {sig_item_2 …}; {carried weapon: length px, width px, angle, position}.
1 px dark outline tinted from each area's own shadow colour, no anti-aliasing, no dithering. Light from the top left, shadows on the lower right.
```
Slots:
- `{race_noun}`: human, dwarf, elf, halfling, gnome, half-elf, half-orc, tiefling, dragonborn. `{sex_noun}`: man / woman (dwarf man, elf woman …).
- `{class_noun}` per §5.
- H, W, C, `h_*`, `heads` per §6.
- `dy_hand` = round(0.36 × H); `dx_hand` = W/2 + 1.
- Ramps per §5/§6.
- `N_colours` 32 [TUNE].

**Facing phrases** (animations only; the base generates all 8 directions):
- S: "facing the viewer"
- W: "facing left, left side profile"
- E: "facing right, right side profile"
- N: "facing away from the viewer"

### 4.2 State templates (`edit_description`, imperative; always end with the KEEP clause)
**KEEP clause (append to every state):**
```
Keep {his|her} face, hair, body proportions, outfit, colours and 1 px self-tinted outline exactly the same; the figure stays {H} px tall with the feet on the same baseline and centred on the same column. Everything stays inside a 48 x 48 px box around the feet.
```
- **S-attack:**
  ```
  Change to an attack pose with the {weapon}: {attack_pose}. The {weapon} is {L_w} px long ({part lengths}), {width} px wide, held in {his|her} {hand} hand at ({dx_grip},{dy_grip}) and angled {angle} from vertical toward {direction}; {offhand item: shape, px, position, material, hex}. Feet {stance_px} px apart. + KEEP
  ```
- **S-cast:**
  ```
  Change to a spellcasting pose: {cast_gesture}. {focus item: shape, px, position, hex}; {glow: none | a {g} px {glow_hex} glow at (dx,dy)}. Weapon {sheathed|slung|in the other hand as in the base}. + KEEP
  ```
- **S-ranged:**
  ```
  Change to a ranged attack pose: {ranged_action}. The {ranged_weapon} is {L_r} px {long|wide}, held at ({dx},{dy}) with {his|her} {hand} hand, aimed {aim}; {ammo: px, colour}. + KEEP
  ```
- **S-down:**
  ```
  Change to lying unconscious on the ground on {his|her} back, body stretched horizontally with the head to the viewer's left, arms loose at the sides, eyes closed; the body is {H} px long and at most {W_down} px tall from the ground, lowest pixel on the feet baseline, centred on the same column; {weapon} lies on the ground beside {him|her}, {L_w} px long. Keep {his|her} face, hair, outfit, colours and 1 px self-tinted outline exactly the same. Everything stays inside a 48 x 48 px box.
  ```
  `W_down` = W + 2.
- **S-work:**
  ```
  Change to a working pose: {work_action}. The {work_tool} is {L_t} px long, held at ({dx},{dy}) in {his|her} {hand} hand; {workpiece: shape, px, position, colour}. Weapon {sheathed|slung as in the base}. + KEEP
  ```

### 4.3 Animation templates (`action_description`: movement only, plus pixel constraints)
**Constraint tail (append to every animation):**
```
The feet stay on the baseline, the head and body keep their size, the {item} stays exactly {L} px long, and nothing leaves the 48 x 48 px box around the feet.
```
- **A-idle** (base, 4 f): `standing idle breathing in 4 frames: the chest and shoulders rise 1 px and fall back; the arms sway 1 px; the feet stay planted.` + tail (item = "sheathed weapon").
- **A-walk** (base, 4 f): `walking in place in 4 frames: frame 1 left foot forward, frame 2 passing with both feet under the body and the head 1 px higher, frame 3 right foot forward, frame 4 passing; the arms swing opposite to the legs by 2 px.` + tail.
- **A-attack** (Attack state, 6 f): `{class attack motion} in 6 frames: frame 1 ready pose, frames 2-3 wind-up {wind_up}, frame 4 strike {strike}, frame 5 follow-through {follow}, frame 6 return to the ready pose.` + tail.
- **A-cast** (Cast state, 6 f): `{cast motion} in 6 frames: frame 1 ready, frames 2-3 {gather}, frame 4 {release}, frames 5-6 return to ready.` + tail. A glow of at most {g} px, only if the state has one.
- **A-ranged** (Ranged state, 6 f): `{ranged motion} in 6 frames: frame 1 ready, frames 2-3 {draw_or_wind}, frame 4 {release}, frames 5-6 recover to ready.` + tail.
- **A-down** (Down state, 6 f, `custom_start_frame` = base standing, `end_frame` = Down): `collapsing in 6 frames: frame 1 standing, frame 2 knees buckle, frame 3 falls to the knees, frame 4 tips backward, frame 5 hits the ground, frame 6 lying still; the {weapon} falls beside the body.` + tail (feet clause becomes: the lowest pixel ends on the baseline).
- **A-work** (Work state, 6 f, loop): `{work motion} in 6 frames as a loop: frames 1-2 {raise}, frame 3 {contact}, frames 4-6 {return}; the workpiece stays fixed in place.` + tail.

